ers by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves (doc `pellham-offering`).\n- **The wider market:** Two utility share offerings were cut back in size, so equity appetite looks weak across the sector, not just for Pellham (doc `utility-credit`).\n- **Capex needs:** Pellham plans 2027 capex up 18% and Norvane up 12%, mostly on substations and transformers (doc `utility-capex-2027`). Equity is therefore being asked to cover a growing need.\n- **Debt as the alternative:** Bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp. Norvane still priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably (doc `utility-credit`), so debt is more costly but still open.\n\n**Implication:** Expect capex to be funded more through debt, and expect some of it to slip. Pellham's cuts are the clearest sign of that slippage, and they hit grid-equipment demand directly.\n\n**Limits of the evidence:** I found only three documents, with no time-series data. I couldn't check whether stronger utilities such as Norvane could still raise equity, or what valuations or discounts would be needed. The \"mostly no\" conclusion rests on one withdrawn deal and two downsized ones.","ttft_ms":2702,"type":"result","duration_ms":19285,"uuid":"05073ea7-958b-4112-b83b-41a5f5c5aa33","ttft_stream_ms":1188,"time_to_request_ms":91,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":5,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":26,"input_hooks":4,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":7,"init_frame_send":5,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":11,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":6},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":40,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":79,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":204,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":131,"process_rs